('d3bb3d8e-9faf-5179-b2bd-7c7dce103727', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 26, 26, 'A grating with 500 lines per mm shows a line in the first order at 19.5°. Calculate the photon energy of this line in eV. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.862', 'eV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m. λ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ × sin 19.5° = 6.676 × 10⁻⁷ m. E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (6.676 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 1.86 eV. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: d. C1: λ = d sin θ. C1: E = hc/λ in eV. A1: E = 1.86 eV.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('df136e6d-4a8c-57c0-af06-39bab2c71272', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 27, 27, 'Electrons with 10.5 eV of kinetic energy collide with hydrogen atoms in the ground state (levels −13.6, −3.40, −1.51 eV). Which line can then be emitted?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Only the line from −3.40 eV to −13.6 eV', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Only the 10.2 eV excitation is possible with 10.5 eV. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: 10.2 eV ≤ 10.5 eV < 12.09 eV. C1: one transition back. A1: C (Only the line from −3.40 eV to −13.6 eV).', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgbd3024d1-t fgbd3024d1-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgbd3024d1-t">Hydrogen energy levels</title><desc id="fgbd3024d1-d">Energy level diagram for hydrogen with levels at −13.6 eV (ground state), −3.40 eV and −1.51 eV.</desc><line x1="110" y1="258" x2="290" y2="258" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="258" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 1</text><text x="298" y="258" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−13.6 eV</text><line x1="110" y1="77.5" x2="290" y2="77.5" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="77.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 2</text><text x="298" y="77.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−3.40 eV</text><line x1="110" y1="44" x2="290" y2="44" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 3</text><text x="298" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−1.51 eV</text><text x="14" y="12" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">energy / eV</text><line x1="14" y1="266" x2="14" y2="24" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="14,20 17,26.3 11,26.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Energy level diagram for hydrogen with levels at −13.6 eV (ground state), −3.40 eV and −1.51 eV.'),
('9e29f176-6924-59f6-99b0-0fbe3fe35323', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 28, 28, 'The yellow sodium "line" is really two lines, at 589.0 nm and 589.6 nm. Calculate the difference between their photon energies, in units of 10⁻²² J. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.436', '10⁻²² J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E₁ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 589.0 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.3769 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. E₂ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 589.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.3735 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. ΔE = 3.3769 × 10⁻¹⁹ − 3.3735 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.44 × 10⁻²² J. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: both energies. C1: enough significant figures kept. C1: difference. A1: ΔE = 3.44 × 10⁻²² J.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 150" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg8938f842-t fg8938f842-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg8938f842-t">The sodium doublet</title><desc id="fg8938f842-d">A magnified spectrum strip from 588.5 nm to 590.0 nm showing the two sodium lines at 589.0 nm and 589.6 nm.</desc><rect x="40" y="40" width="370" height="50" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="163.3" y1="40" x2="163.3" y2="90" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="163.3" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">589.0</text><line x1="311.3" y1="40" x2="311.3" y2="90" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="311.3" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">589.6</text><line x1="40" y1="102" x2="410" y2="102" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="40" y1="102" x2="40" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="40" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">588.5</text><line x1="163.3" y1="102" x2="163.3" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="163.3" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">589.0</text><line x1="286.7" y1="102" x2="286.7" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="286.7" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">589.5</text><line x1="410" y1="102" x2="410" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="410" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">590.0</text><text x="225" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">wavelength / nm</text></svg>', 'A magnified spectrum strip from 588.5 nm to 590.0 nm showing the two sodium lines at 589.0 nm and 589.6 nm.'),
('cb1f1ab0-3fc7-5fe2-867d-002c31807a47', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 29, 29, 'Cool hydrogen gas, with nearly all its atoms in the ground state, is placed in front of a hot ultraviolet source. Which absorption lines are strongest?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Those of the series ending on the ground state (in the ultraviolet)', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Absorption starts from the occupied level, here n = 1. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: atoms in ground state. C1: transitions start from n = 1. A1: A (Those of the series ending on the ground state (in the ultraviolet)).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('cad28725-42ff-50fd-9121-a6e968b09e94', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 30, 30, 'A line in the ultraviolet spectrum of hydrogen has a wavelength of 102.6 nm. Calculate its photon energy in eV. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '12.12', 'eV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (102.6 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 12.1 eV. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = hc/λ. C1: J to eV. A1: E = 12.1 eV.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('8b970524-468a-55fc-bd6f-7fb0d7d4df36', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 31, 31, 'A mercury lamp emits lines at 436 nm and 546 nm. Calculate (photon energy of the 436 nm line) ÷ (photon energy of the 546 nm line).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.252', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E ∝ 1/λ, so ratio = 546 ÷ 436 = 1.25. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = hc/λ. C1: inverse ratio. A1: ratio = 1.25.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 150" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg0bcd809a-t fg0bcd809a-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg0bcd809a-t">Two mercury lines</title><desc id="fg0bcd809a-d">Spectrum strip from 400 nm to 700 nm showing mercury lines at 436 nm and 546 nm.</desc><rect x="40" y="40" width="370" height="50" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="84.4" y1="40" x2="84.4" y2="90" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="84.4" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">436</text><line x1="220.1" y1="40" x2="220.1" y2="90" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="220.1" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">546</text><line x1="40" y1="102" x2="410" y2="102" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="40" y1="102" x2="40" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="40" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">400</text><line x1="163.3" y1="102" x2="163.3" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="163.3" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">500</text><line x1="286.7" y1="102" x2="286.7" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="286.7" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">600</text><line x1="410" y1="102" x2="410" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="410" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">700</text><text x="225" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">wavelength / nm</text></svg>', 'Spectrum strip from 400 nm to 700 nm showing mercury lines at 436 nm and 546 nm.'),
('cedda9bf-553c-5ea0-9d49-fab794f77b46', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 32, 32, 'A hydrogen atom in the −0.85 eV level returns to the ground state (−13.6 eV) in two steps, by way of the −3.40 eV level. Calculate the total energy, in eV, of the two photons emitted.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '12.75', 'eV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Total = (3.40 − 0.85) + (13.6 − 3.40) = 13.6 − 0.85 = 12.75 eV. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: each photon energy. C1: sum equals overall drop. A1: E = 12.75 eV.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 288.9" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgf22c11e8-t fgf22c11e8-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgf22c11e8-t">Hydrogen energy levels</title><desc id="fgf22c11e8-d">Energy level diagram for hydrogen with two arrows: from −0.85 eV down to −3.40 eV, then from −3.40 eV down to the ground state at −13.6 eV.</desc><line x1="110" y1="266.9" x2="290" y2="266.9" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="266.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 1</text><text x="298" y="266.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−13.6 eV</text><line x1="110" y1="95.7" x2="290" y2="95.7" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="95.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 2</text><text x="298" y="95.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−3.40 eV</text><line x1="110" y1="64" x2="290" y2="64" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="64" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 3</text><line x1="110" y1="44" x2="290" y2="44" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="102" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 4</text><text x="298" y="44" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">−0.85 eV</text><line x1="170" y1="44" x2="170" y2="89.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="170,95.7 166.2,87.6 173.8,87.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="230" y1="95.7" x2="230" y2="260.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="230,266.9 226.2,258.8 233.8,258.8" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="14" y="12" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">energy / eV</text><line x1="14" y1="274.9" x2="14" y2="24" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="14,20 17,26.3 11,26.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/></svg>', 'Energy level diagram for hydrogen with two arrows: from −0.85 eV down to −3.40 eV, then from −3.40 eV down to the ground state at −13.6 eV.'),
('486f59d5-777d-5028-8d2a-025c4f3375e9', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 33, 33, 'Hydrogen has a level at −0.38 eV. Calculate the wavelength, in nm, of the line emitted when an electron falls from this level to −3.40 eV. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '411.6 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ΔE = 3.40 − 0.38 = 3.02 eV. λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (3.02 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 4.116 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 4.116 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 412. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: energy gap. C1: λ = hc/ΔE. A1: λ = 412 nm.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('26d90bb4-e807-5670-9423-a88a33312cc4', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 34, 34, 'The spectrum of light from a star shows dark lines at exactly the wavelengths of hydrogen''s visible emission lines. What does this show?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Hydrogen in the star''s cooler outer layers absorbs those wavelengths', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Absorption lines occur at the same wavelengths as the element''s emission lines. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: absorption. C1: same wavelengths as emission. A1: D (Hydrogen in the star''s cooler outer layers absorbs those wavelengths).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('dadb5b33-ac3c-595f-a808-ca7df18b494d', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 35, 35, 'A grating with 400 lines per mm is used to view the 486 nm and 656 nm lines of hydrogen. Calculate the angle between the two lines in the first order, in degrees.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.003', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = 2.5 × 10⁻⁶ m. θ₁ = sin⁻¹ (486 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 2.5 × 10⁻⁶) = 11.21°. θ₂ = sin⁻¹ (656 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 2.5 × 10⁻⁶) = 15.21°. Difference = 15.21 − 11.21 = 4.00°. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: d. C1: both angles. C1: difference. A1: Δθ = 4.00°.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 150" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg098b369c-t fg098b369c-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg098b369c-t">Two hydrogen lines</title><desc id="fg098b369c-d">Spectrum strip from 400 nm to 700 nm showing the two hydrogen lines at 486 nm and 656 nm.</desc><rect x="40" y="40" width="370" height="50" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="146.1" y1="40" x2="146.1" y2="90" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="146.1" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">486</text><line x1="355.7" y1="40" x2="355.7" y2="90" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="355.7" y="28" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">656</text><line x1="40" y1="102" x2="410" y2="102" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="40" y1="102" x2="40" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="40" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">400</text><line x1="163.3" y1="102" x2="163.3" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="163.3" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">500</text><line x1="286.7" y1="102" x2="286.7" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="286.7" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">600</text><line x1="410" y1="102" x2="410" y2="107" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="410" y="118" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">700</text><text x="225" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">wavelength / nm</text></svg>', 'Spectrum strip from 400 nm to 700 nm showing the two hydrogen lines at 486 nm and 656 nm.'),
('39520424-c087-5169-9237-176ca757add8', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 36, 36, 'Why does a neon sign glow red-orange rather than white?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Neon''s energy levels give strong lines mainly in the red-orange part of the spectrum', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The colour comes from the photon energies of neon''s strongest transitions. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: characteristic levels. C1: strong red lines. A1: B (Neon''s energy levels give strong lines mainly in the red-orange part of the spectrum).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2c94255a-d58a-5aa4-bdf9-99c96f74b9d7', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 37, 37, 'Helium has a bright yellow line at 587.6 nm. Calculate the energy difference between the levels involved, in units of 10⁻¹⁹ J. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.385', '10⁻¹⁹ J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'ΔE = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 587.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.38 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ΔE = hc/λ. C1: substitution. A1: ΔE = 3.38 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('93c5a3cd-b05f-5223-a39e-b45f324d3c62', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 38, 38, 'The voltage across a hydrogen discharge tube is increased, so the electrons gain more energy between collisions. What change is seen in the spectrum?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'More lines appear, because higher levels can now be reached', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Line wavelengths are fixed by the levels; more energetic collisions populate more levels. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: wavelengths fixed. C1: higher levels excited. C1: extra lines. A1: C (More lines appear, because higher levels can now be reached).', 4, NULL, NULL),
('e63007b4-643b-5c58-96ef-ffd707a1397a', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 39, 39, 'A grating has slits 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m apart. What is the highest order in which the 589 nm sodium line can be seen?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'n ≤ d/λ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 589 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.396. Orders 1, 2 and 3 have sin θ below 1, so the highest order is 1 + 1 + 1 = 3. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: sin θ ≤ 1. C1: whole number below d/λ. A1: n = 3.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('916cc616-2cb2-5c48-8cc8-77a98e3a5219', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 40, 40, 'What minimum accelerating potential difference gives electrons enough energy to ionise ground-state hydrogen atoms (−13.6 eV) by collision?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '13.6', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'eV = 13.6 eV, so V = 13.6 ÷ 1 = 13.6 V. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ionisation energy 13.6 eV. C1: eV = Ek. A1: V = 13.6 V.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('d326191e-2f32-597a-830a-203cc1c645c1', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 41, 41, 'Why are the wavelengths of the lines of an element the same whatever the source, from a laboratory lamp to a distant star at rest?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They depend only on the energy levels of that element''s atoms', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λ = hc/ΔE and ΔE is fixed by the atom. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: λ = hc/ΔE. C1: levels fixed for the element. A1: A (They depend only on the energy levels of that element''s atoms).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('48e6865e-76bd-5f39-a5ae-3bcec0485cf9', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '8e9dd5f1-dd78-4ce4-8525-76c12991e03f', 'a-level', '28.7', '9702 coursebook 28.7 · Explaining the origin of line spectra', 42, 42, 'A grating with 500 lines per mm shows an unknown yellow line in the second order at 36.2°. (a) Calculate the slit spacing. (b) Calculate the wavelength of the line in nm. (c) Calculate its photon energy and suggest which element produced it. Give only the answer to (b).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '590.6 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) d = 1 ÷ 500 × 10³ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m. (b) λ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ × sin 36.2° ÷ 2 = 5.906 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 5.906 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 591. (c) E = hc/λ ≈ 2.10 eV, which is the sodium line. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: d. M1: nλ = d sin θ with n = 2. C1: value. B1: sodium identified. A1: λ = 591 nm.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg530b5e8a-t fg530b5e8a-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg530b5e8a-t">Second-order line</title><desc id="fg530b5e8a-d">A grating with 500 lines per mm; the unknown line appears in the second order at 36.2° to the straight-through beam (the first order lies between).</desc><line x1="20" y1="140" x2="140" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="110,140 102.7,143.4 102.7,136.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="30" x2="140" y2="250" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="36" x2="144" y2="36" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="44" x2="144" y2="44" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="52" x2="144" y2="52" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="60" x2="144" y2="60" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="68" x2="144" y2="68" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="76" x2="144" y2="76" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="84" x2="144" y2="84" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="92" x2="144" y2="92" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="100" x2="144" y2="100" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="108" x2="144" y2="108" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="116" x2="144" y2="116" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="124" x2="144" y2="124" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="132" x2="144" y2="132" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="140" x2="144" y2="140" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="148" x2="144" y2="148" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="156" x2="144" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="164" x2="144" y2="164" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="172" x2="144" y2="172" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="180" x2="144" y2="180" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="188" x2="144" y2="188" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="196" x2="144" y2="196" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="204" x2="144" y2="204" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="212" x2="144" y2="212" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="220" x2="144" y2="220" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="228" x2="144" y2="228" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="236" x2="144" y2="236" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="244" x2="144" y2="244" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="358" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:4 3"/><text x="362" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 0</text><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="342.9" y2="77.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="348.3,75.