al" style="fill:currentColor">1</text><line x1="20" y1="86.7" x2="24" y2="86.7" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="86.7" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2</text><line x1="20" y1="34" x2="24" y2="34" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="17" y="34" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3</text><text x="203" y="224" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">(1/λ) / 10⁶ m⁻¹</text><text x="20" y="14" font-size="14" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">V / V</text><line x1="324.7" y1="49" x2="332.7" y2="57" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="324.7" y1="57" x2="332.7" y2="49" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="292.8" y1="63.2" x2="300.8" y2="71.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="292.8" y1="71.2" x2="300.8" y2="63.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="262.7" y1="76.9" x2="270.7" y2="84.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="262.7" y1="84.9" x2="270.7" y2="76.9" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="247.3" y1="84.2" x2="255.3" y2="92.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="247.3" y1="92.2" x2="255.3" y2="84.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/></g></svg>', 'Table of LED wavelength and switch-on voltage, and a graph of V against 1/λ: the points lie on a straight line through the origin.'),
('449b3fb2-baa1-5bbc-acc6-66072f832b4d', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 43, 43, 'An X-ray tube works at 40 kV with a beam current of 2.0 mA, and 1.0% of the electrons'' energy becomes X-rays. (a) Calculate the maximum photon energy in keV. (b) Calculate the shortest wavelength. (c) Calculate the X-ray power. Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10⁻¹¹ m. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.108', '10⁻¹¹ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 40 keV. (b) λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (40 × 10³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 3.11 × 10⁻¹¹ m. (c) Input power = 40 × 10³ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 80 W; X-ray power = 0.010 × 80 = 0.80 W. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: max energy = eV. M1: λ = hc/E. C1: value. C1: input power VI. C1: 1% of it. A1: λ(min) = 3.11 × 10⁻¹¹ m.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('0e93d866-417e-5798-8b1c-e94b195bbed8', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 44, 44, 'Sunlight of intensity 1000 W m⁻² reaches the ground. Treat it as photons of wavelength 500 nm. (a) Calculate the photon energy. (b) Calculate the number of photons striking 1.0 m² each second. (c) Explain why the real number is larger. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.514e21', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 500 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.978 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. (b) n = 1000 ÷ 3.978 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.51 × 10²¹. (c) Much of the power is infrared, whose photons carry less energy, so more photons are needed. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: photon energy. M1: n = P/E. C1: value. B1: infrared photons less energetic. A1: n = 2.51 × 10²¹.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('39c27b76-2a0e-5824-822d-20e66f4c987c', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 45, 45, 'A dark-adapted eye responds when 5 photons of wavelength 510 nm arrive within 0.10 s. (a) Calculate the photon energy. (b) Calculate the energy delivered. (c) Calculate the mean power delivered, in units of 10⁻¹⁷ W. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.95', '10⁻¹⁷ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 510 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.900 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. (b) 5 × 3.900 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.950 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. (c) P = 1.950 × 10⁻¹⁸ ÷ 0.10 = 1.95 × 10⁻¹⁷ W. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: photon energy. C1: total energy. M1: P = E/t. C1: value. A1: P = 1.95 × 10⁻¹⁷ W.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('1daedacd-3850-5164-bb63-e6a0cec06e55', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 46, 46, 'A photomultiplier tube turns each photon of 420 nm light that it detects into a pulse of 1.0 × 10⁶ electrons. It detects 1000 photons per second. (a) Calculate the light power detected. (b) Calculate the mean output current. Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10⁻¹⁰ A. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.6', '10⁻¹⁰ A', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = 1000 × 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 420 × 10⁻⁹ = 4.74 × 10⁻¹⁶ W. (b) I = 1000 × 1.0 × 10⁶ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁰ A. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: photon energy. C1: power. M1: charge per second. C1: I = ne. B1: gain explained. A1: I = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ A.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('dfdaadda-4f57-5332-ae88-f378ff30520d', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 47, 47, 'The mean thermal energy of a molecule at 300 K is of order kT. (a) Calculate kT. (b) Calculate the energy of a 500 nm photon. (c) Calculate (photon energy) ÷ kT. Give only the answer to (c). Take k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '96.09', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) kT = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 = 4.14 × 10⁻²¹ J. (b) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 500 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.978 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. (c) 3.978 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 4.14 × 10⁻²¹ = 96.1. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: kT. C1: photon energy. M1: ratio. C1: value. A1: ratio = 96.1.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('616cae0d-068c-525a-a188-5924115bd09e', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 48, 48, 'A radio telescope receives 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ W from a hydrogen cloud at the 21 cm line (1.42 GHz). (a) Calculate the photon energy. (b) Calculate the number of photons received per second. (c) Explain why radio astronomers treat the signal as a wave. Give only the answer to (b). Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.062e9', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 1.42 × 10⁹ = 9.415 × 10⁻²⁵ J. (b) n = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 9.415 × 10⁻²⁵ = 1.06 × 10⁹. (c) About a billion photons arrive each second, each with tiny energy, so the signal is effectively continuous. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: E = hf. C1: GHz to Hz. M1: n = P/E. C1: value. B1: huge numbers of tiny quanta. A1: n = 1.06 × 10⁹.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('0a187003-b496-52f1-84f2-8ce1b85f45a0', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 49, 49, 'A gamma camera detects photons of energy 140 keV from technetium-99m. (a) Convert this energy into joules. (b) Calculate the wavelength in units of 10⁻¹² m. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '8.879', '10⁻¹² m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 140 × 10³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.24 × 10⁻¹⁴ J. (b) λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.24 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 8.88 × 10⁻¹² m. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: keV to J. M1: λ = hc/E. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: λ = 8.88 × 10⁻¹² m.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('e46465b8-d816-5a6d-af01-97a09df4073d', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', 'd5ab6308-3e9f-4910-b58c-36e18c1c8758', 'a-level', '28.8', '9702 coursebook 28.8 · Photon energies', 50, 50, 'A blue LED emits 2.0 × 10¹⁷ photons per second of wavelength 450 nm when it takes 25 mA at 3.2 V. (a) Calculate the light power. (b) Calculate the electrical input power. (c) Calculate the efficiency as a percentage. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '110.5', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = 2.0 × 10¹⁷ × 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 450 × 10⁻⁹ = 0.08840 W. (b) 25 × 10⁻³ × 3.2 = 0.080 W. (c) 0.08840 ÷ 0.080 × 100 = 110 %. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: light power. C1: P = VI. M1: efficiency ratio. C1: value. A1: efficiency = 110%.', 5, NULL, NULL)
