('1d3a384b-b2f7-574c-9c2c-1adccb631f91', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 16, 16, 'Which properties do photons have in common with particles?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They carry definite amounts of energy and momentum', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Each photon carries energy E = hf and momentum p = h/λ, like a particle, but it has no rest mass or charge. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (They carry definite amounts of energy and momentum).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('c589ed2c-ddd3-52af-b584-7c063cfe886a', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 17, 17, 'A 1.0 µW beam of light of wavelength 700 nm falls on a detector. How many photons arrive per second? Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.519e12', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 700 × 10⁻⁹ = 2.841 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. n = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 2.841 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.52 × 10¹². Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: n = P/E. A1: n = 3.52 × 10¹².', 2, NULL, NULL),
('6125811d-e43d-5e9b-89b7-678a8678b873', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 18, 18, 'Newton thought light was a stream of corpuscles. Which observation could his model not explain?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Interference and diffraction patterns', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Patterns of bright and dark need waves superposing. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (Interference and diffraction patterns).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('b7d4e719-b4c3-5ec4-9805-c31b8afad445', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 19, 19, 'Light of wavelength 550 nm falls on a grating with 300 lines per mm. Calculate the first-order angle in degrees.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.497', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = 1 ÷ 300 × 10³ = 3.333 × 10⁻⁶ m. sin θ = 550 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 3.333 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.1650, θ = sin⁻¹ 0.1650 = 9.50°. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: d sin θ = λ. A1: θ = 9.50°.', 2, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgb18a158b-t fgb18a158b-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgb18a158b-t">Grating with 300 lines per mm</title><desc id="fgb18a158b-d">Light of wavelength 550 nm falls normally on a grating with 300 lines per mm; the first-order beams leave at angle θ.</desc><line x1="20" y1="140" x2="140" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="110,140 102.7,143.4 102.7,136.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="30" x2="140" y2="250" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="36" x2="144" y2="36" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="44" x2="144" y2="44" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="52" x2="144" y2="52" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="60" x2="144" y2="60" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="68" x2="144" y2="68" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="76" x2="144" y2="76" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="84" x2="144" y2="84" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="92" x2="144" y2="92" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="100" x2="144" y2="100" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="108" x2="144" y2="108" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="116" x2="144" y2="116" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="124" x2="144" y2="124" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="132" x2="144" y2="132" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="140" x2="144" y2="140" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="148" x2="144" y2="148" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="156" x2="144" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="164" x2="144" y2="164" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="172" x2="144" y2="172" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="180" x2="144" y2="180" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="188" x2="144" y2="188" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="196" x2="144" y2="196" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="204" x2="144" y2="204" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="212" x2="144" y2="212" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="220" x2="144" y2="220" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="228" x2="144" y2="228" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="236" x2="144" y2="236" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="244" x2="144" y2="244" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="358" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:4 3"/><text x="362" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 0</text><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="349.5" y2="105" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="355,104 348.4,108.6 347.3,101.9" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="359" y="100" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 1</text><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="349.5" y2="175" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="355,176 347.3,178.1 348.4,171.4" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><path d="M210,140 A70,70 0 0,0 209,128.4" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="225.7" y="132.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">θ</text><text x="140" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">grating</text><text x="140" y="266" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">300 lines per mm</text></svg>', 'Light of wavelength 550 nm falls normally on a grating with 300 lines per mm; the first-order beams leave at angle θ.'),
('15d8ce9d-4407-57c7-a74d-0755f079172c', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 20, 20, 'In the photon picture, what does a bright fringe in a two-slit pattern represent?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'A place where photons are most likely to arrive', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The wave intensity gives the probability of detecting a photon. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (A place where photons are most likely to arrive).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('fe90ad22-a62f-59fa-b583-bdd873e66d16', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 21, 21, 'Calculate the momentum of a photon of wavelength 450 nm in units of 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.473', '10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'p = h/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 450 × 10⁻⁹ = 1.47 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: p = h/λ. A1: p = 1.47 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('b0a8b542-f08e-5648-8295-15bd8b2abe95', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 22, 22, 'Which phenomenon does NOT give evidence for the wave nature of light?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The photoelectric effect', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Photoelectric emission is explained by photons. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (The photoelectric effect).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('20c9aa01-0223-5381-9e33-a132f8fa63bd', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 23, 23, 'A photon of visible light has an energy of 2.0 eV. Express this in units of 10⁻¹⁹ J. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.2', '10⁻¹⁹ J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 2.0 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: E = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', 1, NULL, NULL),
('b84d950a-95ff-5544-8ca5-13ffc67644bd', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 24, 24, 'In a single-photon two-slit experiment, light of wavelength 633 nm has a power of 1.0 × 10⁻¹³ W. How many photons pass through the apparatus each second? Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.183e5', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 633 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.142 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. n = 1.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 3.142 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.18 × 10⁵. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: photon energy. C1: n = P/E. A1: n = 3.18 × 10⁵.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('c793e745-fb91-5f82-96ed-5ea444cedc69', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 25, 25, 'A beam of 633 nm light of power 1.0 × 10⁻¹³ W carries photons one after another. Calculate the mean time between successive photons, in µs. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.142 us', 'µs', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'n = 1.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 3.142 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.183 × 10⁵ s⁻¹. Mean interval = 1 ÷ 3.183 × 10⁵ = 3.142 × 10⁻⁶ s. In µs: 3.142 × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁶ = 3.14. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: photon rate. C1: interval = 1/rate. C1: s to µs. A1: t = 3.14 µs.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('3e5b9c26-d920-59c0-ba19-ba01165a638c', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 26, 26, 'In a very dim two-slit experiment only one photon is in the apparatus at a time, yet fringes still form. What does this show?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Each photon''s behaviour is described by a wave passing through both slits, which gives the probability of where it lands', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The wave predicts the pattern; each detection is a single photon. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: wave gives probability. C1: single photon detection. A1: A (Each photon''s behaviour is described by a wave passing through both slits, which gives the probability of where it lands).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('d6c30362-44ce-52b0-94d9-9717743e3ea6', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 27, 27, 'Two slits are lit first with blue light (450 nm) and then with red light (650 nm). Calculate (blue fringe spacing) ÷ (red fringe spacing).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.6923', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'x ∝ λ, so ratio = 450 ÷ 650 = 0.692. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: x = λD/a. C1: same a and D. A1: ratio = 0.692.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('10265354-93b6-520f-b38e-33f5fe0785ef', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 28, 28, 'X-rays of wavelength 0.15 nm fall on a crystal. Treat a row of atoms 0.28 nm apart as a diffraction grating and calculate the first-order angle in degrees.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '32.39', '°', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'sin θ = λ/d = 0.15 ÷ 0.28 = 0.5357, so θ = sin⁻¹ 0.5357 = 32.4°. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: d sin θ = λ. C1: same units. A1: θ = 32.4°.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('0dacf53d-53d5-57ca-986d-8bbd31bb1398', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 29, 29, 'A student says the photoelectric effect proves light is only a stream of particles. What is the best reply?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Light also shows interference, so both models are needed: they describe different aspects of its behaviour', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Wave–particle duality: neither model alone describes all observations. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: interference evidence. C1: both models needed. A1: D (Light also shows interference, so both models are needed: they describe different aspects of its behaviour).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('dbc4b936-7130-5296-a27c-74467511a0ef', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 30, 30, 'Light from a star of intensity 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻² and wavelength 550 nm enters an eye through a pupil of diameter 7.0 mm. How many photons enter per second? Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '10642', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'A = π × (3.5 × 10⁻³)² = 3.848 × 10⁻⁵ m². P = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 3.848 × 10⁻⁵ = 3.848 × 10⁻¹⁵ W. n = 3.848 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 3.616 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.06 × 10⁴. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: pupil area. C1: n = P/E. A1: n = 1.06 × 10⁴.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('602153e2-b284-5106-83a6-3c910d8c6e69', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 31, 31, 'A 60 W lamp gives out 3.0 W of visible light, treated as photons of 560 nm, equally in all directions. How many photons pass through each square metre per second at 2.0 m from the lamp? Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.68e17', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'n = 3.0 ÷ 3.552 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 8.446 × 10¹⁸ s⁻¹. Area = 4π × 2.0² = 50.27 m². Flux = 8.446 × 10¹⁸ ÷ 50.27 = 1.68 × 10¹⁷. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: photon rate. C1: sphere area 4πr². C1: flux. A1: flux = 1.68 × 10¹⁷.', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 380 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg6df7c06a-t fg6df7c06a-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg6df7c06a-t">Light spreading from a lamp</title><desc id="fg6df7c06a-d">A lamp radiating 3.0 W of visible light equally in all directions; the light crosses a sphere of radius 2.0 m centred on the lamp.</desc><path d="M269.2,235 A230,230 0 0,0 269.2,5" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><line x1="82.8" y1="125.7" x2="121" y2="142.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="124.8,144.4 118.8,144.5 120.9,139.9" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="83.7" y1="122.9" x2="124.6" y2="131.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="128.7,132.5 122.8,133.8 123.9,128.9" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="84" y1="120" x2="125.8" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="130,120 124.6,122.5 124.6,117.5" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="83.7" y1="117.1" x2="124.6" y2="108.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="128.7,107.5 123.9,111.1 122.8,106.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="82.8" y1="114.3" x2="121" y2="97.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="124.8,95.6 120.9,100.1 118.8,95.5" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><circle cx="70" cy="120" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="70" y="146" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3.0 W visible</text><rect x="296" y="110" width="10" height="20" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="70,166 76.3,163 76.3,169" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="74.9" y1="166" x2="295.1" y2="166" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="300,166 293.7,169 293.7,163" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="185" y="178" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2.0 m</text></svg>', 'A lamp radiating 3.0 W of visible light equally in all directions; the light crosses a sphere of radius 2.0 m centred on the lamp.'),
('cc7cf873-8991-5bd5-a34d-735aa17e4bce', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 32, 32, 'In the wave model the intensity of light is proportional to amplitude². What corresponds to intensity in the photon model?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The number of photons per second per unit area, times the photon energy', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'I = (photon flux) × hf. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: photon flux. C1: × hf. A1: B (The number of photons per second per unit area, times the photon energy).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ecb78cf9-a847-56fe-9608-e44236940e05', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 33, 33, 'A photographic grain needs 1.0 × 10⁻¹² J of 450 nm light to darken. How many photons is that? Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.262e6', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 450 × 10⁻⁹ = 4.420 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. N = 1.0 × 10⁻¹² ÷ 4.420 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.26 × 10⁶. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: photon energy. C1: N = E(total)/E. A1: N = 2.26 × 10⁶.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('1c8c13d2-bde2-5c3e-818e-ee4d4edbe2a7', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 34, 34, 'Maxwell''s theory predicted waves of electric and magnetic fields travelling at 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹. Why was this important for the nature of light?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It matched the measured speed of light, showing light is an electromagnetic wave', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The predicted speed equalled c, uniting optics and electromagnetism. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: predicted speed = c. C1: light is an EM wave. A1: C (It matched the measured speed of light, showing light is an electromagnetic wave).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2ceee7e7-36fd-532b-9366-433b953afcc9', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 35, 35, 'Ten fringe spacings measure 12.0 mm on a screen 0.80 m from two slits 0.40 mm apart. Calculate the wavelength in nm.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '600 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'x = 12.0 ÷ 10 = 1.2 mm. λ = ax/D = 0.40 × 10⁻³ × 1.2 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.80 = 6.000 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 6.000 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 600. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: one fringe spacing. C1: λ = ax/D. A1: λ = 600 nm.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg8da50ee2-t fg8da50ee2-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg8da50ee2-t">Measuring fringes</title><desc id="fg8da50ee2-d">Two slits 0.40 mm apart and a screen 0.80 m away; ten fringe spacings on the screen measure 12.0 mm in total.</desc><rect x="18" y="104" width="56" height="24" rx="3" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="46" y="142" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">laser</text><line x1="74" y1="116" x2="150" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="34" x2="150" y2="103" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="111" x2="150" y2="121" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="129" x2="150" y2="200" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="30" x2="382" y2="204" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="24.1" width="14" height="7.8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="45.7" width="14" height="8.6" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="67.3" width="14" height="9.4" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="88.9" width="14" height="10.2" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="110.5" width="14" height="11" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="132.9" width="14" height="10.2" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="155.3" width="14" height="9.4" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="177.7" width="14" height="8.6" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="200.1" width="14" height="7.8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="107" x2="382" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="150" y1="125" x2="382" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><text x="160" y="86" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0.40 mm</text><line x1="158" y1="92" x2="152" y2="107" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="150,220 156.3,217 156.3,223" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="154.9" y1="220" x2="377.1" y2="220" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="382,220 375.7,223 375.7,217" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="266" y="232" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0.80 m</text><polygon points="372,160 375,166.3 369,166.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="372" y1="164.9" x2="372" y2="177.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="372,182 369,175.7 375,175.7" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="366" y="171" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">x</text><text x="150" y="22" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">double slit</text><text x="382" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">screen</text></svg>', 'Two slits 0.40 mm apart and a screen 0.80 m away; ten fringe spacings on the screen measure 12.0 mm in total.'),
('1c613301-77ed-55db-95ce-d129871fd5a8', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 36, 36, 'A very weak 500 nm beam has a power of 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ W. Calculate the average distance between successive photons along the beam, in km. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '119.3 km', 'km', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'n = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 3.978 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2514 s⁻¹. Spacing = c/n = 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 2514 = 1.193 × 10⁵ m. In km: 1.193 × 10⁵ ÷ 10³ = 119. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: photon rate. C1: spacing = c ÷ rate. C1: m to km. A1: spacing = 119 km.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('35579eb5-21a0-5c0e-8972-f5f9692430bc', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 37, 37, 'When X-rays scatter from electrons, their wavelength increases. Which model explains this best?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Photons colliding with electrons and transferring momentum and energy', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'This is Compton scattering: a particle-like collision. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: photon momentum. C1: energy lost so λ increases. A1: A (Photons colliding with electrons and transferring momentum and energy).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('fc46d8fc-4591-5f2a-a554-b94cc087cb9e', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 38, 38, 'At what speed would an electron have the same wavelength as 550 nm light? Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1323', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = h/mλ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ (9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 550 × 10⁻⁹) = 1320 m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: λ = h/mv. C1: rearranged. A1: v = 1320 m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('cb1ee4d6-ccd5-5db9-8249-b42e3164f587', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 39, 39, 'Which statement best summarises the modern view of light?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Light travels as a wave but exchanges energy in photons', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Propagation is wave-like; emission and absorption are quantised. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: wave propagation. C1: quantised interaction. A1: D (Light travels as a wave but exchanges energy in photons).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('41af6921-b704-519f-a850-bfd03c5caf22', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 40, 40, 'The second-order image of a 546 nm mercury line is at 40.0° for a grating. Calculate the number of lines per mm on the grating.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '588.6', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = 2 × 546 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ sin 40.0° = 1.699 × 10⁻⁶ m. Lines per mm = 1 ÷ (1.699 × 10⁻⁶ × 10³) = 589. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: d = nλ/sin θ. C1: lines per mm. A1: N = 589.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgd0cd6611-t fgd0cd6611-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgd0cd6611-t">Second-order image</title><desc id="fgd0cd6611-d">The 546 nm mercury line leaves a grating in the second order at 40.0° to the straight-through beam.</desc><line x1="20" y1="140" x2="140" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="110,140 102.7,143.4 102.7,136.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="30" x2="140" y2="250" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="36" x2="144" y2="36" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="44" x2="144" y2="44" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="52" x2="144" y2="52" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="60" x2="144" y2="60" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="68" x2="144" y2="68" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="76" x2="144" y2="76" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="84" x2="144" y2="84" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="92" x2="144" y2="92" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="100" x2="144" y2="100" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="108" x2="144" y2="108" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="116" x2="144" y2="116" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="124" x2="144" y2="124" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="132" x2="144" y2="132" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="140" x2="144" y2="140" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="148" x2="144" y2="148" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="156" x2="144" y2="156" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="164" x2="144" y2="164" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="172" x2="144" y2="172" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="180" x2="144" y2="180" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="188" x2="144" y2="188" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="196" x2="144" y2="196" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="204" x2="144" y2="204" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="212" x2="144" y2="212" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="220" x2="144" y2="220" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="228" x2="144" y2="228" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="236" x2="144" y2="236" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="244" x2="144" y2="244" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="358" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:4 3"/><text x="362" y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 0</text><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="341.1" y2="71.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="346.4,69.9 340.7,75.5 338.5,69.1" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="350.4" y="65.9" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 1</text><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="341.1" y2="208.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="346.4,210.1 338.5,210.9 340.7,204.5" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="266.8" y2="33.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="271.1,30 267.7,37.3 263.4,32.1" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="275.1" y="26" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">n = 2</text><line x1="140" y1="140" x2="266.8" y2="246.4" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="271.1,250 263.4,247.9 267.7,242.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><path d="M210,140 A70,70 0 0,0 193.6,95" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="180.3" y="57.3" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">40.0°</text><line x1="178.3" y1="63.3" x2="201.1" y2="101.8" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="140" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">grating</text></svg>', 'The 546 nm mercury line leaves a grating in the second order at 40.0° to the straight-through beam.'),
('4993d636-4afc-5d93-9782-54db45fe561a', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 41, 41, 'A photon passes through a two-slit apparatus. When does it behave most like a particle?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'When it is detected at the screen', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Detection and absorption are localised events. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: detection localised. C1: travel described by wave. A1: B (When it is detected at the screen).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ed9b18e5-dc69-5523-bb88-675bc594649f', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 42, 42, 'In a version of Taylor''s experiment, a two-slit pattern is photographed with light of 550 nm and power 2.0 × 10⁻¹³ W. (a) Calculate the number of photons per second. (b) The apparatus is 1.5 m long. Calculate the mean number of photons inside it at any instant. (c) Comment on the result. Give only the answer to (b). Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.002765', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) n = 2.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 3.616 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 5.530 × 10⁵ s⁻¹. (b) Transit time = 1.5 ÷ 3.00 × 10⁸ = 5.0 × 10⁻⁹ s; number = 5.530 × 10⁵ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁹ = 0.00277. (c) Far fewer than one, so photons pass one at a time, yet fringes form. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: photon rate. C1: transit time. M1: number = rate × time. B1: less than one photon at a time. A1: N = 0.00277.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('b0927b33-b0e4-58a7-b131-36f725ae17a7', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 43, 43, 'A two-slit pattern of 633 nm light builds up on a detector that records 3.0 × 10⁵ photons per second. A clear pattern needs about 1.0 × 10⁶ photons. (a) Calculate the light power reaching the detector. (b) Calculate the time needed for a clear pattern. Give only the answer to (a), in units of 10⁻¹⁴ W. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.427', '10⁻¹⁴ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 633 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.142 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; P = 3.0 × 10⁵ × 3.142 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 9.43 × 10⁻¹⁴ W. (b) t = 1.0 × 10⁶ ÷ 3.0 × 10⁵ = 3.3 s. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: photon energy. M1: P = nE. C1: value. C1: t = N/n. B1: pattern builds statistically. A1: P = 9.43 × 10⁻¹⁴ W.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('a1638871-f5f4-54d7-a087-d89fbddf54b1', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 44, 44, 'A 1.0 mW helium–neon laser (632.8 nm) has a beam of diameter 1.0 mm. (a) Calculate the intensity. (b) Calculate the photon energy. (c) Calculate the number of photons crossing each square metre of the beam per second. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.051e21', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) I = 1.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ (π × (0.50 × 10⁻³)²) = 1273 W m⁻². (b) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 632.8 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.143 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. (c) 1273 ÷ 3.143 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 4.05 × 10²¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: beam area. C1: intensity. C1: photon energy. M1: flux = I/E. A1: flux = 4.05 × 10²¹.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('a7f1d2dd-9903-511f-ba39-5c6c4625f08f', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 45, 45, 'Light of intensity 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ W m⁻² falls on a metal of work function 2.0 eV. In a wave model, each electron can absorb energy only through an area of 1.0 × 10⁻²⁰ m². (a) Calculate the power absorbed by one electron. (b) Calculate the time it would need to gain 2.0 eV. (c) Contrast this with what is observed. Give only the answer to (b), in s. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.6e5', 's', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 1.0 × 10⁻²⁰ = 2.0 × 10⁻²⁴ W. (b) t = 2.0 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁴ = 1.60 × 10⁵ s. (c) Emission is observed within nanoseconds, so energy must arrive in photons. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: P = IA. C1: eV to J. M1: t = E/P. C1: value. B1: no delay observed. A1: t = 1.60 × 10⁵ s.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('f43faa9b-8a66-53a2-ae22-d18b23ee7a8b', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 46, 46, 'X-rays scattered by a crystal whose atomic rows are 0.30 nm apart give a first-order maximum at 14.5°. Treat the rows as a grating. (a) Calculate the wavelength. (b) Calculate the photon energy in keV. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '16.55', 'keV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) λ = d sin θ = 0.30 × 10⁻⁹ × sin 14.5° = 7.511 × 10⁻¹¹ m. (b) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 7.511 × 10⁻¹¹ = 2.648 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. In keV: 2.648 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁶ = 16.5. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: λ = d sin θ. C1: photon energy. M1: J to keV. C1: value. A1: E = 16.5 keV.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('1a9a423b-439f-5651-bdfe-bbdb0553a96a', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 47, 47, 'Two slits 0.30 mm apart give fringes 2.1 mm apart on a screen 1.2 m away. (a) Calculate the wavelength. (b) Calculate the photon energy in eV. (c) Calculate the number of photons per second in a 0.20 mW beam. Give only the answer to (b). Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.368', 'eV', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) λ = ax/D = 0.30 × 10⁻³ × 2.1 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.2 = 5.250 × 10⁻⁷ m. (b) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (5.250 × 10⁻⁷ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 2.37 eV. (c) n = 0.20 × 10⁻³ ÷ (E in J) ≈ 5.28 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: λ = ax/D. C1: photon energy in J. M1: in eV. C1: value. C1: photon rate. A1: E = 2.37 eV.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg4c4c7b75-t fg4c4c7b75-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg4c4c7b75-t">Two-slit fringes</title><desc id="fg4c4c7b75-d">Two slits 0.30 mm apart produce bright fringes 2.1 mm apart on a screen 1.2 m away.</desc><rect x="18" y="104" width="56" height="24" rx="3" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="46" y="142" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">laser</text><line x1="74" y1="116" x2="150" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="34" x2="150" y2="103" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="111" x2="150" y2="121" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="129" x2="150" y2="200" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="382" y1="30" x2="382" y2="204" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="24.1" width="14" height="7.8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="45.7" width="14" height="8.6" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="67.3" width="14" height="9.4" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="88.9" width="14" height="10.2" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="110.5" width="14" height="11" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="132.9" width="14" height="10.2" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="155.3" width="14" height="9.4" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="177.7" width="14" height="8.6" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="385" y="200.1" width="14" height="7.8" rx="0" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="107" x2="382" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><line x1="150" y1="125" x2="382" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:3 3"/><text x="160" y="86" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">0.30 mm</text><line x1="158" y1="92" x2="152" y2="107" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="150,220 156.3,217 156.3,223" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="154.9" y1="220" x2="377.1" y2="220" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="382,220 375.7,223 375.7,217" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="266" y="232" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.2 m</text><polygon points="372,160 375,166.3 369,166.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="372" y1="164.9" x2="372" y2="177.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="372,182 369,175.7 375,175.7" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="366" y="171" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2.1 mm</text><text x="150" y="22" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">double slit</text><text x="382" y="18" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">screen</text></svg>', 'Two slits 0.30 mm apart produce bright fringes 2.1 mm apart on a screen 1.2 m away.'),
('4899c1b2-2944-5cf7-b3b3-f0f1e3e9785c', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 48, 48, 'Sunlight at Mars has an intensity of 590 W m⁻². Treat it as photons of mean wavelength 550 nm. (a) Calculate the photon energy. (b) Calculate the number of photons arriving on each square metre per second. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.631e21', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 550 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.616 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. (b) n = 590 ÷ 3.616 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.63 × 10²¹. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: photon energy. M1: n = I/E. C1: value. B1: per m² per s. A1: n = 1.63 × 10²¹.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('1dcb8e29-9309-5c71-bdff-cf810908247c', '7d22edfc-44c4-4846-9769-292936cb7827', '7ea4a576-84c8-4a0f-9474-20a442190683', 'a-level', '28.9', '9702 coursebook 28.9 · The nature of light: waves or particles?', 49, 49, 'A grating with 600 lines per mm shows the sodium line in the second order at 45.0°. (a) Calculate the wavelength in nm. (b) Calculate the photon energy in eV. (c) Calculate the photon momentum. Give only the answer to (a). Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '589.3 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) d = 1 ÷ 600 × 10³ = 1.667 × 10⁻⁶ m; λ = 1.667 × 10⁻⁶ × sin 45.0° ÷ 2 = 5.893 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 5.893 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 589. (b) ≈ 2.11 eV. (c) p = h/λ ≈ 1.13 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: d. M1: nλ = d sin θ. C1: value. C1: photon energy. C1: photon momentum. A1: λ = 589 nm.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgc312f6f6-t fgc312f6f6-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgc312f6f6-t">Sodium line, second order</title><desc id="fgc312f6f6-d">A grating with 600 lines per mm shows the sodium line in the second order at 45.0° to the straight-through beam.</desc><line x1="20" y1="140" x2="140" y2="140" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="110,140 102.7,143.4 102.7,136.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="30" x2="140" y2="250" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="36" x2="144" y2="36" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="136" y1="44" x2="144" y2="44" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-li